.TH CVEC_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_redeclare \- declare the companion type variables again for a vector from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_redeclare(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares the two companion type variables for v. The
type-dispatching macros read those variables. The macro does not declare v
itself, and it does not touch the vector that v names. Use this macro
wherever a cvec comes from somewhere else. A function parameter is the
usual case. A struct member that you copy into a local is another case. You
can then use cvec_at(3), cvec_push(3) and the other macros on that handle.

The companion variables carry only type information. Nothing reads them at
run time, they are marked unused, and they cost nothing. The value
companion carries the element type. The type-dispatching macros use it to
type their result, and to size what they copy. The key companion carries
size_t. That is the type of the index which a vector iterator reports
through ccol_iter_key_ptr(3).

The type here is a statement about the vector. It is not a request to
change the vector. Nothing compares it against the element size that
created the vector. A different type therefore reinterprets the stored
bytes through that type.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable that is in scope already
type  Element type that the vector holds
      Any type name is accepted, including a function
      pointer type such as void (*)(int) and an array
      type such as int[4]. For an array element type,
      give cvec_push(3) an array or a string literal.
      The macro copies its bytes into the element
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a pair of declarations. It needs a semicolon at the
end of the call site. It therefore belongs where declarations are legal.
It cannot be the body of an if or a loop without braces.
The macro pastes v into the names of the companion variables. v must
therefore be a plain identifier.
The macro declares nothing with the name v. You cannot use it on a handle
that is not in scope already. It creates no vector and destroys none.
A second use for the same v in the same scope declares the same names
again, and the code does not compile.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The type here must be the real element type of the vector. The compiler
reports no diagnostic for a wrong type. The type-dispatching macros then
read and write the bytes of the vector as that type. For a type of a
different size, they also compute element offsets that do not match the
offsets of the vector itself.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process(cvec vec) {
    cvec_redeclare(vec, int);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < cvec_size(vec); i++) {
        printf("%d\en", cvec_at(vec, i));
    }
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_at (3),
.BR cvec_push (3),
.BR cvec_size (3),
.BR ccol_iter_key_ptr (3)
